      {
        "position": 1,
        "title": "Great for mosquito fogger, perimeter defense",
        "text": "I spend a lot of time outside with my outdoor \"porch cats,\" and got this natural spray primarily for perimeter defense against insects. My two cats follow me around all day, and \"help\" with yard work and in my workshop (or at least they think they're helping). So I look for a natural insect solutions that will work for all three of us lol.My favorite use of this Kate's Home & Garden Spray for Dogs & Cats (Rosemary) is using it as a localized fogger to clear out active mosquitoes.It is highly effective, -- a quick misting into the air keeps mosquitoes away for about 10 to 20 minutes.After field-testing several formulas from this brand, I found all of them perform well, but I feel the Rosemary blend is slightly more effective for the tiger mosquitoes in my area.The Peppermint option works well, but any drifting mist can cause a mild burning sensation on the skin and especially the eyes.Regardless of which formula, the cats don't like it on their bedding, and avoid areas I've sprayed. So it defeats the purpose of treating their bedding if they avoid anything I've directly sprayed.However, they'll tolerate me misting/fogging the air, and using it as a perimeter defense on the porch leaves a residual layer on the porch floor and surfaces that provides a mild, lingering deterrent effect.I also combine this treatment with secondary barriers like personal bug spray and citronella candles.I measured the output patterns at a fixed distance of 3 feet using two different measuring methods: a tape measure and a folding wood ruler.On the narrow stream setting, the spray pattern stays highly focused and measures exactly 6 inches in diameter at that 3-foot mark.Switching over to the wide spray setting yields a broader mist pattern that measures 12 inches in diameter at the same 3-foot distance.A standout feature is the natural and non-toxic approach this brand uses.The only ingredient I dislike is sodium lauryl sulfate, which I generally prefer to avoid.Otherwise, this formula offers a very solid overall value as a clean, natural approach to backyard pest management.",
        "rating": 5,
        "date": "July 14, 2026",
        "author": "Customer Review",
        "author_link": "https://www.amazon.com/gp/profile/amzn1.account.AEGQBJGUCJNUU5CGE6CI7HQFZB7Q?ref=cm_cr_dp_d_bdcrb_top",
        "author_image": "https://m.media-amazon.com/images/S/amazon-avatars-global/f7ca574d-e914-4d6b-a271-3bbdf291f021._CR0%2C0%2C500%2C500_SX460_SX48_.jpg"
      },
      {
        "position": 2,
        "title": "Seems to work decently well",
        "text": "I got this for our camping trips to help keep fleas and ticks away from our pets. The scent isn’t as rosemary-forward as I expected, I noticed more of a eucalyptus smell. It’s definitely a strong scent, which seems to help repel fleas, although I found I needed to reapply because it didn’t seem to last very long.I can’t really speak to how well it works against ticks since I haven’t noticed enough to judge, but it did seem to help reduce fleas. Overall, it’s a good option if you’re looking for a more natural alternative to traditional bug sprays. Only negative is the bottle leaks. I have to be careful with it.",
        "rating": 4,
        "date": "July 17, 2026",
        "author": "Amazon Customer",
        "author_link": "https://www.amazon.com/gp/profile/amzn1.account.AEIWUUENL6HAMGG75RLU3OYKKB4A?ref=cm_cr_dp_d_bdcrb_top",
        "author_image": "https://m.media-amazon.com/images/S/amazon-avatars-global/default._SX48_.png",
        "images": [
          "https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/71cu937dmSL.jpg"
        ]
      },
      {
        "position": 3,
        "title": "Smells Feirce, But Actually Seems To Work!",
        "text": "I got this spray as a last resort of sorts for our cattledog. He has allergies to fleas, but no matter what medication we use from the veterinarian, he still picks some up from outside and ends up itchy.This sprays out in a pretty fine mist, but you'll want to either spray it on their coat and work it in, or spray it on a cloth and work it in. After testing him to make sure it wouldn't cause him any issues, I decided to spray directly onto his coat.This spray does have a very strong rosemary scent, like very strong. If you're someone who doesn't like the scent of rosemary, you'll want to avoid this.While this is strong, I find it 100% worth it. This is the first time in a LONG time that he isn't itching. He seems totally comfortable, and for that I could cry.If you find yourself with a furry friend who still seems miserable even on medication, this is definitely something to try!!",
        "rating": 5,
